  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was Memorial Day \u2014 a structural 30% revenue headwind \u2014 and both stores performed within expectation given the holiday. Port Washington held flat at $3,384 despite the calendar hit, and Hicksville's 22% decline tracks the expected holiday dip. What matters: neither location broke down on a forced-rest day.\n\nThe real story is capacity. Port Washington ran 6 groomers yesterday at $476/groomer, 8% below the trailing-30-day baseline of $518. Hicksville ran 2 groomers at $342/groomer, down 11% from its $386 baseline. Both stores are running softer per-groomer productivity than their own norms, and add-on attach is down across the board \u2014 Port Washington at 46% vs 56% last week, Hicksville at 40% vs 50%. This is an upsell discipline signal, not a holiday artifact. You should be proud of Port Washington's retail pivot: up 35% while transactions fell 18% means your team is selling smarter at the front of house.\n\nMonth-to-date, Port Washington is down 4% vs April, but calendar-adjusted it's only 3% behind expectation \u2014 performance is real but modest. Hicksville is actually beating DOW-adjusted expectation by 1.2%, a genuine bright spot. Year-to-date, Port Washington is up 55% and Hicksville is 10 weeks in; the trajectory is solid. The concern: visit-cadence drift at Port Washington (12+ week bucket at 15.3%) suggests customers are spacing out. Monitor whether this accelerates post-holiday.\n\n**Capacity & coverage:** Today you have 8 groomer-days across both stores (6 Port Washington, 2 Hicksville) with 7 bather hours at Port Washington; tomorrow 7 groomer-days with 7 bather hours Port Washington; next 7 days: 40 groomer-days total, 43 bather hours."
